Q: Why does my Java while loop keep repeating even after entering the correct value? A: <p>The loop condition is actually working correctly. The reason <code>&quot;Incorrect password&quot;</code> is still printed is because that line runs immediately after reading the input, before the loop checks the condition again.</p>
<p>One simple way to fix it is to only print the message when the password is actually wrong:</p>
<pre><code>while (!userInput.equals(password)) {

    System.out.println(&quot;Enter password:&quot;);
    userInput = input.nextLine();

    if (!userInput.equals(password)) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Incorrect password&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>That way, the message is skipped when the correct password is entered and the program goes directly to <code>&quot;Access granted&quot;</code>.</p>

Q: Error: function timed out, ensure the promise resolves within XXX, due to promises in SEQUENTIAL using reduce/Promise.resolve(nightwatch cucumber) A: <p>You should check them in parallel batches. The issue is that your sequential promise chain times out. Each element check waits up to 60 seconds (due to a large dataset), and this cumulative time exceeds Cucumber's step timeout limit.</p>
<pre><code>const checkAllInParallel = async () =&gt; {
    const promises = sheetData.map(async (record, index) =&gt; {
        try {
            await page.load(param);
            await page.waitForPageLoad();
            await client.waitForElementVisible(record[&quot;Locator&quot;], 10000);

            return { 
                status: &quot;fulfilled&quot;, 
                index: index + 1, 
                locator: record[&quot;Locator&quot;] 
            };
        } catch (error) {
            return { 
                status: &quot;rejected&quot;, 
                index: index + 1, 
                locator: record[&quot;Locator&quot;],
                error: error.message 
            };
        }
    });

    return await Promise.allSettled(promises);
};
</code></pre>

Q: How to crop image and upload using ng-file-upload? A: <p>Pass the newly cropped Image in your function.</p>

<pre><code>&lt;button type="submit" ng-click="submit(cropper.croppedImage)"&gt;submit&lt;/button&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Now change the submit function accordingly:</p>

<pre><code>$scope.submit = function(croppedImage) {
    $scope.upload(croppedImage);
};
</code></pre>
